        <!--
             THIS SERVICE NEEDS CLOVER `PAYMENTS_R` PERMISSION TO BIND
             ========================================================================================
             REAL-TIME DISCOUNT (RTD) PROVIDER SERVICE CONFIGURATION
             ========================================================================================
             To act as an RTD Provider, a service MUST:
             1. Be exported.
             2. Handle the "com.clover.REALTIME_DISCOUNT_PROVIDER" intent action.
             3. Declare constraints via <meta-data> tags. A provider is ONLY invoked if the current
                transaction matches at least one of its declared constraints.

             CONSTRAINT TYPES & PRECEDENCE:
             When Clover resolves which provider to call, it evaluates constraints in this order of precedence:

             [Precedence 1] 'wallet': Matches the digital wallet used.
                 - Probable format values: GOOGLE_PAY, APPLE_PAY, SAMSUNG_PAY, DEFAULT
             [Precedence 2] 'cardTypeWithRange': Matches the card brand AND a specific BIN range.
                 - 'cardType' must exactly match the Clover CardType enum string.
                 - 'lowBin' and 'highBin' define the inclusive alphanumeric range for the card's first 6 digits.
             [Precedence 3] 'cardType': Matches any card of the specified brand.
                 - Probable format values: VISA, MC, AMEX, DISCOVER, DINERS_CLUB, JCB, MAESTRO, SOLO, LASER,
                   CHINA_UNION_PAY, CARTE_BLANCHE, UNKNOWN, GIFT_CARD, EBT, GIROCARD, INTERAC, RUPAY, EFTPOS, BANCNET, OTHER

             HOW TO DESIGN YOUR CONSTRAINTS:
             - AT LEAST ONE CONSTRAINT IS REQUIRED. However, you do NOT need to implement all three 
               types (`wallet`, `cardType`, `cardTypeWithRange`). You can add or omit any of them as 
               needed for your specific discount logic, as long as your service defines at least one.
             - Be as specific as possible. If your discount only applies to Visa cards issued by a specific bank,
               use 'cardTypeWithRange' rather than 'cardType'. This prevents your service from being unnecessarily
               bound and queried for transactions it cannot discount, reducing checkout latency.
             - Constraints are defined as JSON arrays of objects. You can define multiple rules within the same
               metadata tag (e.g., value='[{"wallet":"GOOGLE_PAY"},{"wallet":"APPLE_PAY"}]').
             - If multiple installed providers match the same transaction at the same precedence level, 
               the Clover system's selection is non-deterministic. Cooperative, non-overlapping constraints are ideal.

             EXAMPLES:
             <meta-data android:name="wallet" android:value='[{"wallet":"GOOGLE_PAY"}]' />
             <meta-data android:name="cardTypeWithRange" android:value='[{"cardType":"VISA","lowBin":"400000","highBin":"499999"}]' />
             <meta-data android:name="cardType" android:value='[{"cardType":"GIFT_CARD"}]' />
             ========================================================================================
        -->
        <service android:name=".rtd.ExampleRealtimeDiscountProviderService"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.clover.REALTIME_DISCOUNT_PROVIDER" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="cardTypeWithRange"
                android:value='[{"cardType":"DINERS_CLUB","lowBin":"360705","highBin":"379999"}]' />
        </service>
